  "summary": "Round 7's DependencyGraph class and decoupled resolution/invalidation design are the strongest proposals yet, but three concrete issues remain unresolved. First, the DependencyGraph class is initialized 'once per auto-run cycle' but the auto() function in pipeline.py doesn't have a single auto-run cycle — it processes features across four separate stage-priority passes (implementing, review, ideating, approved) per board, and within each pass a feature's stage can change (e.g., review pass moves features to implementing or final-human-approval), meaning the graph's id-to-stage snapshot becomes stale mid-cycle; the graph either needs to be rebuilt between passes or expose a mutation method to update stage mappings as transitions occur, otherwise is_blocked() will return stale results for features whose dependencies were just completed in the same cycle. Second, the plan_invalidation_check() triggered on rejection transitions creates an infinite loop risk: if feature A depends on B and B is rejected, plan_invalidation_check() moves A back to plan-inbox — but the auto() function's next cycle will pick A up for re-planning, and if the re-planner produces a new plan that still references B (since depends_on is 'never mutated by stage transitions'), A will pass is_blocked() (rejected counts as resolved) and proceed to implementation with a plan that may still assume B exists; the invalidation check either needs to clear or flag the stale depends_on entry, or the re-planning prompt must explicitly surface which dependencies were rejected so the planner can adapt. Third, the WebUI integration via the three-touch-point pattern (api.go handler → WebSocket message → TUI dispatch) ignores the dual communication architecture: the codebase has both ServerClient (WebSocket-based, in server_client.py) and PipelineClient (Unix socket IPC, in client.py), and the TUI now uses PipelineClient for commands while ServerClient handles legacy WebSocket relay — adding edit_depends_on requires deciding which path owns the mutation, and the answer is that it must go through PipelineClient.send_command() to the headless pipeline service (pipeline/service.py), which owns the FeatureFile instances, not through the WebSocket relay to the TUI process. The discussion has not yet addressed this dual-client architecture at all."
